{
  "title": "Recently played shortcut on the cabinet home page",
  "open_questions": [
    {
      "id": "memory-scope",
      "question": "Is it acceptable for the cabinet to remember your recent games only in the browser you played them in (so a different device or a cleared browser starts fresh), or must the list follow a person across devices?",
      "required": true
    },
    {
      "id": "what-counts-as-played",
      "question": "Does simply opening a game from the cabinet count as having played it, or should it only be remembered after the person actually plays for a while (and if so, roughly how long)?",
      "required": true
    },
    {
      "id": "clearing-history",
      "question": "Do players need a visible way to clear or remove entries from their recently played list in this release, or is that out of scope for now?",
      "required": true
    }
  ],
  "criteria": [
    {
      "id": "recent-section-above-grid",
      "title": "A recently played section appears above the full grid on the cabinet page",
      "guidance": "Open the cabinet page after at least one game has been played. A distinct section appears above the existing grid of all games, headed with wording that says these are the games this person played (e.g. 'Recently played'), not a recommendation. Check the existing markup and rendering code (index.html, src/cabinet.js) first — this repository already has a page structure and the section must slot above it rather than replace it. Verifiable by an automated DOM/render test.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "full-grid-unchanged",
      "title": "The full catalogue is still shown underneath, complete and in its current order",
      "guidance": "With any recent history present, the existing grid still lists every game in src/catalogue.js in the same order and with the same links as it does today. Nothing is removed from or reordered in the full grid because it also appears in the recent section. Compare against the current behaviour covered by tests/cabinet.test.js and tests/catalogue.test.js.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "most-recent-first",
      "title": "Recent games are listed most recently played first",
      "guidance": "Play A, then B, then C; the recent section shows C, B, A in that order. Automated test drives the recording function then renders the section and asserts the order of entries.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "replay-moves-to-front",
      "title": "Playing a game again moves it to the front instead of listing it twice",
      "guidance": "Play A, then B, then A again; the recent section shows A, B once each with A first. No game ever appears more than once in the section. Assert both the order and the absence of duplicates in an automated test.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "cap-at-three",
      "title": "At most three games are shown",
      "guidance": "Play four or more distinct games; the recent section renders exactly three entries — the three most recently played — and the older ones are dropped, not just hidden by styling. Assert the number of rendered entries, not the number stored.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "survives-tab-close",
      "title": "The list survives closing the tab and returning later",
      "guidance": "Recent plays are written to durable per-browser storage (localStorage or equivalent) and re-read on the next page load, so a fresh page load with no in-memory state still renders the same list. Decision taken unless the memory-scope question says otherwise: this is per-browser only, with no accounts or server storage. Test by writing history, discarding all in-memory state, re-initialising from storage, and asserting the same list renders.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "recording-on-launch",
      "title": "Launching a game from the cabinet records it as played",
      "guidance": "Starting a game through the cabinet causes that game's identifier to be recorded at the front of the history. Decision taken unless the what-counts-as-played question says otherwise: opening the game counts, recorded at the moment of launch. Test by simulating a launch and asserting storage now holds that game first.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "survives-broken-state",
      "title": "Broken, missing or unexpected stored data never stops the cabinet loading or a game starting",
      "guidance": "With stored history that is absent, empty, invalid JSON, the wrong shape (a string, a number, an object, an array of non-strings), or absurdly long, the page still renders, every game in the catalogue still appears in the full grid and every game link still works, and no error is thrown to the page. The bad value is treated as no history and the recent section simply does not appear. Test each of these stored values in turn.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "retired-games-ignored",
      "title": "History entries for games that no longer exist are skipped silently",
      "guidance": "If the history names a game that is no longer in src/catalogue.js, it is filtered out: no broken tile, no dead link, no error, and the remaining valid entries still render in order. Test with a history mixing one unknown identifier and two known ones.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "empty-state-hidden",
      "title": "A first-time visitor sees the cabinet exactly as it is today",
      "guidance": "Decision taken: when nothing has been played, the recent section (heading included) is not rendered at all rather than showing an empty placeholder, so the page is unchanged for a new visitor. Assert that no recent-section element exists when history is empty.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "heading-reads-as-yours",
      "title": "The heading and section make it obvious these are the player's own games",
      "guidance": "A person reads the rendered page and judges that the section reads as 'games you played', clearly distinct from a suggestion or promotion, and sits comfortably above the existing grid. Taste judgement — no automated check proposed.",
      "required": true,
      "human": true
    }
  ],
  "gates": [
    {"id": "lint", "run": "npm run lint"},
    {"id": "test", "run": "npm test"}
  ],
  "gate_bindings": [
    {
      "id": "acceptance-recently-played",
      "run": "node --test acceptance/recently-played.test.js",
      "proves": "most-recent-first"
    }
  ],
  "tasks": [
    {
      "id": "recent-history-store",
      "brief": "briefs/recent-history-store.md",
      "dir": ".",
      "objective": "Read src/catalogue.js, src/cabinet.js, index.html and acceptance/recently-played.test.js to learn how games are identified and rendered today, then add a small module that records and reads a per-browser history of played games. Done looks like: a record function that puts a game identifier at the front of the list, removes any earlier occurrence of the same game so it is never duplicated, and keeps at most the three most recent; a read function that returns those entries newest first, resolved against the catalogue so unknown identifiers are dropped; persistence to localStorage (or equivalent) so a fresh load recovers the same list; and every read path defensive, so missing, empty, malformed, wrong-typed or oversized stored values are treated as no history instead of throwing. No page rendering is changed in this task.",
      "objective_note": null
    },
    {
      "id": "render-recent-section",
      "brief": "briefs/render-recent-section.md",
      "dir": ".",
      "objective": "Render the recently played section on the cabinet page above the existing grid, using the store from the previous task. Done looks like: when history is non-empty, a distinct section with a heading that reads as the player's own games shows up to three tiles, most recent first, each linking to the same place its grid tile links to; when history is empty or unusable the section is absent entirely and the page looks exactly as it does today; and the full grid below still lists every catalogue game in its current order and remains fully usable regardless of what the history contains.",
      "objective_note": null
    },
    {
      "id": "record-plays-and-harden",
      "brief": "briefs/record-plays-and-harden.md",
      "dir": ".",
      "objective": "Wire recording into the launch path so that starting a game from the cabinet records it at the front of the history, and prove the whole feature cannot take the arcade down. Done looks like: launching any game updates the stored history; acceptance/recently-played.test.js passes; and tests cover the awkward cases — replay reordering, the three-item cap, survival across a simulated tab close, a history naming a retired game, and each broken stored value (invalid JSON, wrong type, oversized) still leaving the page rendering and every game launchable. npm run lint and npm test both stay green.",
      "objective_note": null
    }
  ]
}
